# The auxiliary ULPI transceiver of a Great Scott Gadgets Cynthion r1.4,
# wired as a USB **serial port with SHA-256 and ChaCha20 behind it**: the
# same thirteen ULPI balls, the same oscillator and the same six LEDs
# `usb_ulpi_device.rcf` and `usb_cdc_uart.rcf` constrain, for the same
# reasons.
#
# `usb_ulpi_device.rcf` has the long form of every line's provenance — the
# platform file's own `ULPIResource`, why `-slew fast` is on all thirteen
# pins and not only the outputs, why no pin gets a `-pullup`, and where A8
# and the six LEDs sit in the fabric. Nothing here differs from it, and it
# is repeated rather than shared because a constraints file is a statement
# about one design's ports and this design has its own.
#
#     ULPIResource("aux_phy", 0,
#         data="F16 G15 G16 H15 J15 J16 K15 K16", clk="D16", clk_dir='o',
#         dir="E16", nxt="F15", stp="E15", rst="J13", rst_invert=True,
#         attrs=Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33", SLEWRATE="FAST")),
#
#     # Primary, discrete 60MHz oscillator.
#     Resource("clk_60MHz", 0, Pins("A8", dir="i"),
#         Clock(60e6), Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33")),
#
#     *LEDResources(pins="E13 C13 B14 A15 D12 C11",
#                   attrs=Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33"), invert=True),
#
# `default_usb_connection = "aux_phy"` is the platform file's own, and
# `apollo_port_sharing` names only `control_phy`, so the AUX port is nobody
# else's: loading this cannot take away the Apollo debugger this board is
# programmed over.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **No VBUS switch.** The two of them are bidirectional onto a shared node
# and `docs/apollo-protocol.md` §7 is about why exactly one may ever be
# enabled; this design needs neither, because the host it answers is on the
# other side of the AUX connector and supplies its own.
#
# **No waveform on a pin.** `usb_cdc_uart.rcf` puts a 115200 baud 8N1
# waveform on ball C11 because that design has a real UART in it. This one
# has no serial line at all — the bytes go from the endpoint to a parser and
# from a parser to two crypto cores — so C11 carries `led5_n`, which is a
# core being busy, and an oscilloscope on it sees how long a command took
# and nothing finer. A **power** trace is what would say anything about what
# the cores leak, and that needs a shunt and not a pin;
# `ip/crypto/sha256/README.md` §5 is clear that nothing in this repository
# has taken one.
#
# The USER button is deliberately **not** constrained, for the same reason
# as next door: a manual reset would need the core's `rst_n`, which is
# asynchronous, and `configure_registers` refuses an asynchronous reset on
# this family.

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk A8

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[0] F16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[1] G15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[2] G16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[3] H15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[4] J15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[5] J16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[6] K15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_data[7] K16

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_dir E16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_nxt F15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_stp E15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_rst_n J13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 -slew fast ulpi_clk D16

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led0_n E13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led1_n C13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led2_n B14
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led3_n A15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led4_n D12
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led5_n C11
